Title:
DefenderKiller — Kernel process termination using Microsoft’s signed KSLDriver.sys (BYOVD)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
DefenderKiller is a proof-of-concept BYOVD-style tool that weaponizes an old Microsoft-signed Defender driver (KSLDriver.sys, 2011) to terminate arbitrary processes from kernel mode. It leverages an IOCTL-exposed primitive that ultimately triggers `ZwTerminateProcess`, enabling termination of PPL-protected and many EDR-protected processes. The workflow includes loading the driver via a service, bypassing an `AllowedProcessName` check by writing a custom path into the registry, and issuing IOCTL `0x222044` (sub-command 8) with a target PID. Because the termination occurs in ring 0, common user-mode protections (e.g., handle access callbacks) are bypassed. This is primarily useful for red teams, pentesters, and malware researchers evaluating EDR resilience and driver trust assumptions. It’s notable because it uses Microsoft’s own signed driver and claims it is not covered by the vulnerable driver blocklist.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD using Microsoft-signed kernel drivers
- Kernel-mode process termination via `ZwTerminateProcess`
- IOCTL interface abuse (`0x222044`, sub-command 8)
- Bypassing PPL (Protected Process Light)
- Bypassing EDR handle protections (`ObRegisterCallbacks`) via kernel `ZwOpenProcess`
- Driver loading/unloading via Windows services + registry-based checks bypass

Use Cases:
- Terminate EDR/AV user-mode processes during authorized engagements
- Validate defensive coverage for vulnerable/legacy signed drivers and blocklist gaps
- Research and reproduce kernel kill primitives exposed via IOCTL handlers
- Build detections around suspicious driver load + IOCTL patterns + registry tampering
- Stress-test EDR self-protection/watchdog respawn behavior

Keywords:
DefenderKiller, BYOVD, KSLDriver.sys, Microsoft Malware Protection, signed driver abuse, IOCTL 0x222044, IRP_MJ_CLEANUP, ZwTerminateProcess, ZwOpenProcess, ring 0, kernel-mode, PPL, Protected Process Light, ObRegisterCallbacks, driver blocklist, Windows service driver load, registry modification, EDR self-protection, process termination primitive